+## 8. Exact inversion: an error target determines minimum delay
+
+Let $0\leq\delta\leq1$ be a target chordal error and put $\varepsilon=\delta^2$. Define $T_{\min}(u,v;\delta)$ as the least actual peak delay among degree-at-most-one routers whose three errors are at most $\delta$, and set it to infinity if no finite router exists. Continue to arrange $u\geq v$. Put
+
+\[
+e_1=\frac{R+uv-1}{2(R+uv)},\qquad R=\sqrt{(1+u^2)(1+v^2)},
+\]
+
+\[
+d=\frac{u+v}{2},\qquad m=\frac{v-u}{2}.\tag{28}
+\]
+
+**Theorem 2 (complete inverse and a cubic certificate).** If $\varepsilon\geq1/2$, then $T_{\min}=0$. If $e_1\leq\varepsilon<1/2$, then $T_{\min}=1$. If $\varepsilon=0$, then $T_{\min}=+\infty$ and no finite router attains the target.
+
+For $0<\varepsilon<e_1$, define
+
+\[
+g=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{\varepsilon}>1,\qquad A_g=1+m^2+(g+1)d^2,
+\]
+
+\[
+G(y)=gy^3-A_g y-2md^2.\tag{29}
+\]
+
+If $m<0$, let $y_0$ be the unique zero of G in $(0,d/\sqrt{g})$; if $m=0$, set $y_0=0$. Then put
+
+\[
+y=\min\left(y_0,\frac{d}{g}\right),\qquad B=-m-gy,
+\]
+
+\[
+C=m^2+2gmy+gy^2+(g-1)d^2,
+\]
+
+\[
+p_{\varepsilon}=\frac{1+C}{2\sqrt{C-B^2}},
+\]
+
+\[
+T_{\min}=p_{\varepsilon}+\sqrt{p_{\varepsilon}^2-1}>1.\tag{30}
+\]
+
+The target is attained with $t=m+y$, $Q(s)=(1-\varepsilon)(s-t)^2$ and $P=H-Q$, using the two-port compiler in Section 6. Thus inversion requires at most one isolated cubic root. The cubic can be skipped when $G(d/g)\geq0$, because then $y=d/g$. Its equality case is exactly the positivity transition; its strict positive case is exactly the active phase.
+
+**Proof.** Each finite-cap value in (3) is positive. On the inactive branch $A(t)$ strictly increases with T at every fixed t, so the minimum of $A(t)/D(t)$ strictly increases: evaluate the smaller-cap function at the larger-cap minimizer to obtain the strict inequality. The active branch is strictly decreasing in squared error because L strictly increases with T. The two expressions are continuous and agree at the sole possible transition. Together with (26), this proves that $e_{\mathrm{opt}}$ is a continuous, strictly decreasing bijection from $[1,\infty)$ onto $(0,e_1]$. The constant branch and the degree-one peak lower bound give the first two cases. Positivity of the finite-cap law gives the zero-target case. For every $0<\varepsilon<e_1$ there is therefore one optimal cap above one, and all three errors equal the target at that cap.
+
+By Theorem 1, its unique optimal Q is a rank-one quadratic with leading coefficient $1-\varepsilon$. Write its zero as $t=m+y$. The two endpoint equalities in (7), together with the normalized leading coefficient of H, force
+
+\[
+H(s)=g(s-t)^2+(g-1)(u+s)(v-s).\tag{31}
+\]
+
+Expanding gives exactly B and C in (30), and
+
+\[
+C-B^2=(g-1)(d^2-gy^2),
+\]
+
+\[
+\det(P_{\mathrm{matrix}})=(1-2\varepsilon)(d^2-g^2y^2).\tag{32}
+\]
+
+Since P has leading coefficient $\varepsilon>0$, global feasibility is equivalent to $|y|\leq d/g$. On that entire closed interval H is positive definite because $g>1$. Conversely, every y in this interval produces a feasible two-port router with exactly the three target errors. This proves that minimizing the delay in this one-parameter family gives the unrestricted optimum: the family contains the optimizer already established by Theorem 1, and every member is feasible.
+
+For a positive definite normalized M the peak of the completion is the square root of its eigenvalue ratio. Its equivalent monotone parameter is
+
+\[
+p(y)=\frac{1+C}{2\sqrt{C-B^2}}.
+\]
+
+It is at least one, with equality precisely when $B=0,C=1$. Direct differentiation gives
+
+\[
+p^{\prime}(y)=-\frac{g(g-1)G(y)}{2[(g-1)(d^2-gy^2)]^{3/2}}.\tag{33}
+\]
+
+If $m<0$, then $G(0)>0$ and
+
+\[
+G\left(\frac{d}{\sqrt{g}}\right)=-\frac{d}{\sqrt{g}}\left[1+(m+d\sqrt{g})^2\right]<0.
+\]
+
+On the positive half interval $G^{\prime\prime}(y)=6gy>0$, so its derivative is strictly increasing and the sign change has exactly one zero. On the negative half interval, writing $y=-s$ gives $G(-s)=s(A_g-gs^2)-2md^2>0$ because $A_g-gs^2>0$. Thus p strictly decreases up to $y_0$ and strictly increases afterward within $(-d/\sqrt{g},d/\sqrt{g})$. If $m=0$, the factorization $G(y)=y(gy^2-A_g)$ gives the same conclusion with minimum at zero. Restricting to $[-d/g,d/g]$ yields the minimum in (30). Since $\varepsilon<e_1$, its peak cannot equal one. This completes the proof.
+
+**Elementary active inverse.** When the constraint in (30) is active or at equality, let
+
+\[
+L_{\varepsilon}=\frac{d(1-2\varepsilon)}{\sqrt{\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon)}}.
+\]
+
+Then (30) simplifies to
+
+\[
+p_{\varepsilon}=\frac{1}{2}\left(L_{\varepsilon}+\frac{1+v^2}{L_{\varepsilon}}\right),
+\]
+
+\[
+T_{\min}=p_{\varepsilon}+\sqrt{p_{\varepsilon}^2-1}.\tag{34}
+\]
+
+Indeed $B=-v$, $C=v^2+L_{\varepsilon}^2$. Equivalently, $L=p+w$ and $p^2-w^2=1+v^2$ invert the active forward law directly. If $u>3v$, the transition target has squared error
+
+\[
+\varepsilon_c=\frac{v^2}{Q_c}.\tag{35}
+\]
+
+For targets below $e_1$, the active inverse applies exactly when $\varepsilon<\varepsilon_c$, with equality at the transition; larger targets use the inactive inverse. Equation (35) follows from $x=\varepsilon/(1-\varepsilon)=v^2/w^2$ at the transition. If $u\leq3v$, every finite inverse below $e_1$ is inactive. For symmetry $u=v=c$, equation (30) reduces further to $T_{\min}=c\sqrt{\varepsilon^{-1}-2}$ for $0<\varepsilon<e_1$.
+
+**Exact inverse checks.** For $u=2,v=1,\varepsilon=35/123$, the isolated cubic root is $y=1/4$, with $g=88/35$, $B=-9/70$ and $C=223/70$. Its minimum delay is
+
+\[
+T_{\min}=\frac{\sqrt{15529}+9\sqrt{53}}{106}=1.7937395977414851561\ldots.\tag{36}
+\]
+
+The rational active fixture in Section 7 at squared target $16/41$ returns minimum delay 2; the boundary fixture at $9/25$ also returns 2 and satisfies $G(d/g)=0$. For $u=3,v=1/3$, equation (35) gives $\varepsilon_c=(8-\sqrt{10})/18$. These are squared targets; the corresponding chordal targets are their positive square roots. All inverse statements retain the original dimensionless delay, degree bound and open-angle assumptions.
+
